id: holding-back
name: Holding Back
type: instant
actionCost: 0
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
resource: holdingback
resourceLabel: Holding Back
resourceMaxFrom: baseTierOfPower
holdingBack: true
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Pull your power down. A stack takes a Tier of Power off and puts a point on your
  Concealment; filling up to your base Tier drops you to Tier 1 and costs 1(bT) on
  every Combat Roll.
text: >
  Holding Back Maneuver [1/Round]: Hold back your power to avoid detection or warm up in
  a fight.
  –Maneuver Type: Instant Maneuver
  –Action Cost: N/A
  –Minions: Free
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Effect: Gain any number of Holding Back Stacks (the maximum you can possess is equal
  to your base Tier of Power). For each stack of Holding Back, reduce your Tier of Power
  by 1. If your number of Holding Back Stacks is equal to your base Tier of Power, reduce
  your Combat Rolls by 1(bT) and set your Tier of Power to 1.

  For each stack of Holding Back you possess, increase the Skill Bonus for your
  Concealment Skill Checks by 1 (max. 3).

  If you already possess a Holding Back Stack, you can instead choose to remove any
  number of them or gain more up to your maximum.

---

# --- The Resource ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "The maximum you can possess is equal to your base Tier of Power." The first Resource in
# this system whose ceiling is not a number the file can write down: it differs per
# character and grows with them. `resourceMaxFrom` names the value to read instead, and
# both places that clamp a Resource have the character to read it off.
#
# No clock. Every other Resource here runs out on a turn edge; these come off when you take
# them off, which is what the entry's last paragraph is for.
#
# --- How many ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Gain any number", and "you can instead choose to remove any number of them or gain more
# up to your maximum." So the Maneuver asks: a number between nothing and your ceiling, and
# what you already hold is where the field starts. Taking them off is the same question
# answered lower, which is why it is one question rather than two - the entry writes it as
# a choice between gaining and removing and they come to the same number.
#
# Asked rather than scripted, because a script has no way to ask a player anything. Set
# rather than added, for the same reason: "remove any number" and "gain more up to your
# maximum" are both a new total.
#
# --- What a stack does ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Three things, and the third replaces the first at the top of the scale.
#
# `tierOfPower` is settled before anything else is, which is why the amounts here are in
# (bT) - the Tier of Power is what (T) means and it is not known yet.
#
# The full-stack case is written as its own branch rather than as a `set` landing on top of
# the subtraction. Both would come to the same number, and one of them says why: at the
# ceiling the entry stops subtracting and states the answer, because subtracting your whole
# base Tier would leave you at nothing and there is no Tier 0.
#
# ">=" rather than "==", which the entry writes. The ceiling makes more than equal
# unreachable through the Maneuver, and a GM who sets the stacks by hand should get the
# floor rather than a Tier of Power below 1 - the reading that cannot go wrong either way.
[passive]
if (holdingback.stacks > 0 && holdingback.stacks < baseTierOfPower) {
    tierOfPower -= holdingback.stacks;
}

if (holdingback.stacks >= baseTierOfPower && holdingback.stacks > 0) {
    tierOfPower = 1;
    combatRolls -= 1(bT);
}

# "Increase the Skill Bonus for your Concealment Skill Checks by 1 (max. 3)." The cap is on
# what the stacks are worth rather than on the stacks - a character with a base Tier of 5
# may hold five and still only gets three.
if (holdingback.stacks > 0) {
    skill.concealment.bonus += min(holdingback.stacks, 3);
}

# --- What is not built ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Avoid detection." What Concealment is rolled against, and who is looking, is the table's.
# The Skill Bonus is the whole of what this can do about it.
#
# "Minions: Free." There are no Minions in this system, which the Arrogant Declaration,
# Analysis, Hype, Brace and Flip files all say about their own Minion lines.
#
# --- How it is gained ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Special Maneuver, and what opens it has not been read yet. Concealment is the Skill this
# entry touches, which is not the same question as the Skill that hands the Maneuver over -
# Dirty Trick rolls Bluff and Bluff opens the Feint instead.
